WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.160
With nearly six decades of TV series and films&nbsp;
under its belt, Star Trek is a vaunted franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:12.000
that always inspires fan discussion on the best&nbsp;
entries in the series. Here’s why Star Trek 6:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.920
The Undiscovered Country is the best&nbsp;
film embodiment of the Star Trek world.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.680
Creator Gene Roddenberry imagined Star Trek&nbsp;
as a "Wagon Train to the Stars," with Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:26.640
settlers boldly exploring the final frontier&nbsp;
of space. But he also conceived of it as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.960
Cold War allegory, one in which the United&nbsp;
Federation of Planets represented NATO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.920
the Klingon Empire stood in for the Soviet Union,&nbsp;
and the Romulans were the Red-Communist Chinese.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.040
A number of episodes of the original series&nbsp;
allegorize Cold War conflicts, including "Errand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:47.040
of Mercy," "Friday's Child," "Elaan of Troyius,"&nbsp;
"The Omega Glory," and "Balance of Terror."

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.600
Producer Harve Bennett's "holy&nbsp;
trilogy" – The Wrath of Khan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.000
The Search for Spock, and The Voyage Home –&nbsp;
explored the relationships and trials of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.120
Enterprise crew and the sacrifices they made for&nbsp;
one another. But when Paramount brought back Wrath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.160
of Khan writer/director Nicholas Meyer to make&nbsp;
one final film with the original series cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.720
he and Leonard Nimoy took Star Trek back to&nbsp;
its Cold War roots, imagining "the fall of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:14.000
the Berlin Wall in space," and its aftermath.&nbsp;
Specifically, they wanted to dramatize the hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.520
work of achieving peace in a world used to hate,&nbsp;
paranoia, and xenophobia. The film’s plot, about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.240
a coup that attempts to derail a galactic peace&nbsp;
conference, mirrored the real events unfolding in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.280
the summer of 1991 so accurately — including&nbsp;
Russian hardliners trying to restore the old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.840
Soviet Union — that Paramount actually tried to&nbsp;
rush the film into theaters before it was ready.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:39.280
Maybe because he started out as a novelist,&nbsp;
Nicholas Meyer has a real gift with dialogue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.280
Sure, he borrows some of the dialogue in his&nbsp;
Star Trek movies from classic works of Western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.400
Civilization, but he's good at using those&nbsp;
works to underscore his themes. Star Trek II,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:54.080
for example, opens with Spock giving Kirk an&nbsp;
antique copy of Dickens's A Tale of Two Cities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.120
a novel about sacrifice. Later in the film, Kirk&nbsp;
quotes the novel, reflecting on Spock's sacrifice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.920
for the greater good. In The Undiscovered Country,&nbsp;
Meyer has the Klingon character Chang lay claim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.280
to Shakespeare and even weaves in references to&nbsp;
Brave New World, Sherlock Holmes, and Peter Pan.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.840
But Meyer also writes excellent original&nbsp;
dialogue. The Undiscovered Country is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.200
about people confronting the prejudice&nbsp;
of their own thinking, and the way in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.000
which Kirk and Spock acknowledge their own&nbsp;
shortcomings and mistakes is very poignant.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.440
"You're a great one for logic. I'm a great one&nbsp;
for rushing in where angels fear to tread."

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.240
A bit later in the film, Spock asks,

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.560
"Is it possible that we two, you and I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.480
have become so old and inflexible&nbsp;
that we have outlived our usefulness?"

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.480
It’s very self-reflective dialogue&nbsp;
that allows for meaningful change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.000
making Undiscovered Country a film&nbsp;
with exciting character development.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.520
A script with plenty of Shakespearean dialogue&nbsp;
demands Shakespearean actors, and Undiscovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.640
Country delivers. Royal Shakespeare Company&nbsp;
veteran David Warner plays Chancellor Gorkon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.440
a Klingon ambassador trying to forge peace with&nbsp;
the Federation. The great Christopher Plummer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.960
meanwhile, stars as the villainous Klingon General&nbsp;
Chang, who spearheads a conspiracy to sabotage the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:10.640
peace process. By this time, Plummer was as famous&nbsp;
as a Shakespearean actor on the stage as he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.920
for his roles on screen. He had made his thespian&nbsp;
bones in Canadian theatre working with none other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:21.280
than William Shatner. The future Captain Kirk&nbsp;
was Plummer's understudy in...wait for it...Henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:26.080
V. So when playing Chang, Plummer is&nbsp;
right at home intoning quotes from Hamlet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:31.120
Henry V, Julius Caesar, and The Merchant&nbsp;
of Venice while he taunts Kirk and company.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.600
"To be, or not to be. That is the question&nbsp;
which preoccupies our people, Captain."

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.760
While Plummer gives a famously&nbsp;
flamboyant performance as Chang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.920
he is fully committed to the role, giving&nbsp;
it the dramatic heft it deserves. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.840
Plummer had been a Star Trek fan&nbsp;
when the show debuted in the 1960s.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.880
Despite the fact that Industrial Light and Magic&nbsp;
handled visual effects for both the Star Wars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.520
and Star Trek movies, their Star Wars effects&nbsp;
became better known, partly because of Trek’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.400
association with low-budget aesthetic in the&nbsp;
original TV show. But the movies featured some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.760
impressive spectacles the space battle inside the&nbsp;
pink clouds of the Mutara Nebula in Wrath of Khan;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.960
the Enterprise self-destructing and&nbsp;
burning up in The Search for Spock;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:14.880
and the Klingon Bird of Prey hovering&nbsp;
over a whaling ship in The Voyage Home.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.680
Undiscovered Country is the most sustained&nbsp;
visual achievement of the original movie series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.920
It outdoes those previous films in several&nbsp;
ways, as with the mammoth opening explosion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:28.080
of the Klingon mining moon and the giant&nbsp;
purple shock wave that engulfs Sulu's ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:33.040
the Excelsior. There's a terrific action&nbsp;
sequence set on a gravity-disabled Klingon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:37.440
flagship when two masked assassins&nbsp;
gun down the Klingons onboard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:42.240
sending their fuchsia blood floating in neon&nbsp;
globules through the zero-G environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:47.680
Star Trek 6 is also bursting with alien masks&nbsp;
and makeup, putting on a huge prosthetic show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.640
and giving the galaxy a sense of being diversely&nbsp;
populated. Finally, Meyer and cinematographer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.680
Hiro Narita elect for low lighting throughout,&nbsp;
which gives the film a burnished, menacing glow.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:02.880
Every Star Trek movie either introduces new&nbsp;
characters or delves deeper into characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.520
from the original TV series, and the quality of&nbsp;
the films often mirrors how well this is done.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.560
Take the arch-villain Khan, who made his classic&nbsp;
cinema debut in Star Trek II, and contrast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:17.840
that with Spock's forgettable half brother,&nbsp;
Sybok, introduced in the wretched Star Trek V.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.720
Star Trek 6 balances out the main cast with&nbsp;
a wealth of memorable new faces beyond Chang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:28.160
and Gorkon, including Gorkon's fierce daughter&nbsp;
Azetbur , played by Rosanna DeSoto, as well as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:33.360
grandfather of Star Trek: The Next Generation's&nbsp;
Worf, both played by Michael Dorn. Other sharply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.600
drawn characters include a shapeshifting&nbsp;
alien named Martia, played by the model Iman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:42.640
and Kurtwood Smith as the Federation President.&nbsp;
Even Christian Slater shows up for a cameo.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:47.440
But the best new character is a pre-Sex and&nbsp;
the City Kim Cattrall as Lieutenant Valeris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:51.440
Spock's Vulcan protégé. Valeris has more&nbsp;
scenes than most of the Trek regulars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.920
and the movie is better for the freshness&nbsp;
and vitality of her character. She and Chang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.800
along with many other new characters, help make&nbsp;
the Star Trek universe feel densely populated.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:06.000
Novelizations and other material in the Trek&nbsp;
universe had long established the promotion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.760
to captain of Sulu, played by George Takei, which&nbsp;
fans had been clamoring to see on film for quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.560
some time. His ship, the USS Excelsior, had&nbsp;
actually been introduced in Star Trek III:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:21.600
The Search for Spock as one of a new generation&nbsp;
of starships that would replace the Enterprise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.680
When Kirk goes rogue in that movie, Starfleet&nbsp;
sends the Excelsior to apprehend him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.800
but it fails because Scotty has sabotaged the&nbsp;
transwarp drive. But fans found the ship cool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.840
and they finally got their wish&nbsp;
of seeing Sulu in charge of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.160
when it showed up under his command a&nbsp;
few films later in Undiscovered Country.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:41.440
Narratively, adding another Starfleet&nbsp;
ship widens the scope of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:45.040
and it gives one of the primary original&nbsp;
characters something to do besides hang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:49.120
around on the Enterprise bridge. Sulu and&nbsp;
the Excelsior feature prominently in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:52.960
dramatic opening of the film,&nbsp;
as well as the exciting climax.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:56.720
As they race at Warp 10 to help the&nbsp;
Enterprise save the galactic peace conference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:00.720
a junior officer warns Sulu about the&nbsp;
speed, to which his commander barks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:04.960
"Fly her apart then!" You can practically&nbsp;
feel Takei's relish at getting a line this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:07.680
exciting after "yes sirring"&nbsp;
Captain Kirk all those years.

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:12.000
Sulu’s expanded role fits well into the&nbsp;
fast, exciting storytelling on display&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.880
in The Undiscovered Country. A Sherlock Holmes&nbsp;
fan, writer-director Meyer – who also wrote The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:21.360
Seven Percent Solution – infuses a mystery&nbsp;
story into the larger space opera plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:25.920
as the Enterprise crew races against the&nbsp;
clock to figure out who has framed them for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.160
act of international terrorism that&nbsp;
jeopardizes a peace conference. Meyer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:35.120
and his co-writers Leonard Nimoy and Denny&nbsp;
Martin Flynn also add a juicy courtroom trial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:39.760
an elaborate banquet scene, and a prison escape&nbsp;
for good measure, along with all the space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:43.840
battles, phaser fights, and philosophical&nbsp;
discussions fans had come to expect.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:49.120
In Star Trek II, Spock saves the Enterprise&nbsp;
and her crew at the expense of his life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:53.440
In Star Trek III, the Enterprise crew&nbsp;
returns the favor but sacrifices the ship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:58.400
In Star Trek 4, the stakes include the survival&nbsp;
of Earth and all its people. And by the time we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:03.360
get to The Undiscovered Country, the fate of the&nbsp;
entire galaxy hangs in the balance. Can humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:08.480
and alien species work together to preserve the&nbsp;
fragile goal of peace? Or is the galaxy doomed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:13.680
to perpetual war? These are the highest stakes&nbsp;
of the series so far, making for great tension.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:17.680
The personal stakes are high in this movie too.&nbsp;
Whereas the characters had to save each other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:21.920
individually in previous outings, in Undiscovered&nbsp;
Country, they’ve got to save themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:26.480
as a group. Will they be able to see the error of&nbsp;
their ways before it's too late? They certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:31.840
try. Spock admits that it was, quote, "arrogant&nbsp;
presumption" on his part that got them into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:36.800
story's mess in the first place, as well as his&nbsp;
belief in the inherent superiority of Vulcans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:40.080
Kirk meanwhile confesses that he&nbsp;
was so used to fighting Klingons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:43.600
it never occurred to him to believe&nbsp;
they might actually want peace. saying,

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:47.360
"Gorkon had to die before I&nbsp;
understood how prejudiced I was."

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:52.240
By the end of the film, he sees them in a&nbsp;
new light and has even earned their trust:

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:54.720
"People can be very frightened of change."

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.320
Inside, the starships in The Undiscovered Country&nbsp;
feel more like tight-packed ships at sea, or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:05.280
submarines, than do the brightly lit flying living&nbsp;
rooms of The Next Generation. This fits with Gene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:10.320
Roddenberry's original vision of Starfleet as a&nbsp;
navy, with the officers using nautical directions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:15.040
like port and starboard. And of course, the USS&nbsp;
Enterprise was meant to be the descendant of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:20.080
many real ships designated "Enterprise," including&nbsp;
the famous World War II aircraft carrier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:25.120
Meyer embraced Roddenberry's vision of Star&nbsp;
Trek as fictional British naval hero Horatio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:30.560
Hornblower voyaging in space. In Star Trek 6,&nbsp;
the director expands that concept even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:34.880
fully than he did in Star Trek II, putting&nbsp;
the crewmen together in cramped bunkrooms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:39.040
referring to the kitchen as the galley,&nbsp;
and using torpedoes during warfare.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:43.440
Meyer epitomizes the naval theme&nbsp;
beautifully with Kirk's great final line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:46.960
When asked for a course heading,&nbsp;
he paraphrases Peter Pan, saying,

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:49.920
"Second star to the right,&nbsp;
and straight on 'til morning."

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:53.600
Of course, Peter Pan was an adventure set&nbsp;
on ships, but the line also suggests the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:57.920
eternal pull of the horizon, which had always&nbsp;
informed the hopeful vision of Star Trek.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:02.800
It’s now expected for popular franchises&nbsp;
to wink at their fans, referencing elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:07.440
outside of a given film's particular story.&nbsp;
But when Star Trek 6 came out in 1991,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:11.760
the practice was pretty rare, and the film&nbsp;
trailblazed in that area too. At one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:16.720
when Scotty suggests that their situation is so&nbsp;
dire that they’re practically dead, Spock quips,

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:18.400
"I've been dead before."

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:23.680
Meanwhile, as the film tackles the theme&nbsp;
of aging, it directly engages the cultural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:27.760
jokes about the Enterprise crew saving&nbsp;
the galaxy in walkers and wheelchairs.

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:32.160
But nods to the franchise’s history are never&nbsp;
at the expense of the film’s central story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:37.120
The only responsibility the filmmakers feel is&nbsp;
to make a good final movie, especially after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:42.320
lackluster Star Trek V. The sixth installment&nbsp;
grandly sends off the original cast and crew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:47.040
but it's also a send-off to the fans who made&nbsp;
it all possible. Their passion saved Star Trek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:51.120
from cancellation and kept it alive through&nbsp;
some pretty weak movies. With Star Trek 6,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:56.080
the original series ends on a high note. Its&nbsp;
greatest gift to fans is saving the best for last.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:00.560
Check out one of our newest videos right here!&nbsp;
Plus, even more Looper videos about Star Trek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:05.677
are coming soon. Subscribe to our YouTube channel&nbsp;
and hit the bell so you don't miss a single one!

